.TH BLAWG 1
.SH NAME
blawg \- an opinionated static site generator
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B blawg
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B blawg
builds a static site from a series of blog posts saved as
.I markdown
files with a
.I YAML
frontmatter containing post metadata, much as is used by
.B pandoc(1)
separated from the body of the post by three dashes
.
.SH EXAMPLES
Here is an example of a blog file:

.nf
.RS
title: Blawg
date: 1/1/1
tags:
  \- unix
  \- vim
\-\-\-

# Blawg

Today I started to write my blawg...
.RE
.fi
.SH SERIES
Posts can be grouped into a multi-part
.I series
(chapters) with two frontmatter fields:
.I series,
the series name (used both as its display title and as the key used to
group posts together), and
.I part,
a 1-based chapter number.

.nf
.RS
title: Part Two
series: My Deep Dive
part: 2
\-\-\-
.RE
.fi

Every post in a series has its full, part-ordered chapter list available
to templates. Two posts in the same series may not share a
.I part
number, and a post with
.I series
set must also set
.I part;
both are build errors. If the template set defines a
.I series-index
block, each series additionally gets an index page at
.I /series/<slug>/.
.SH REQUIREMENTS
Fenced code blocks marked
.I mermaid
are rendered to inline SVG using
.B mmdc(1)
from
.I @mermaid-js/mermaid-cli,
which in turn requires a Chrome/Chromium binary available to Puppeteer.
If
.B mmdc
is not installed or fails, the block falls back to a plain
.I <pre><code>
block.

Install with:
.nf
.RS
npm install -g @mermaid-js/mermaid-cli
npx puppeteer browsers install chrome-headless-shell
.RE
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
pandoc(1), mmdc(1)